expanded_sketch:
In order to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:main-theorem}, the paper “consider[s] separately the cases where $X$ is hyperbolic and where $X$ is not hyperbolic.” The “hyperbolic case follows from the convergence group theorem as discussed above” (with some care if $X$ is hyperbolic but not a Cayley graph).

For the non-hyperbolic case, the “ultimate goal is to prove that non-hyperbolicity plus
\begin{equation}
  \tag{$\rm{UBQ}$}\label{eq:BQ}
  \parbox{4in}{    \strut
    There exists $\sigma > 0$ such that for any connected, bi-infinite quasi-geodesic $\rho \subset X$, we have that $X \setminus N_\sigma(\rho)$ contains exactly two wide connected components. 
    \strut
  }
\end{equation}
 implies that $X$ has quadratic growth.” “From here, a theorem of Trofimov … plus the Bass--Guivarc'h formula … allows us to conclude that $X$ is quasi-isometric to the Euclidean plane.”

To reach quadratic growth, the argument is organized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item Introduce \emph{witnesses} (\S\ref{sec:witness}), “pairs of rays which probe deep into the two wide components bound by a bi-infinite quasi-geodesic.”
\item Use the \emph{witness protection lemma}: “if we perturb a bi-infinite geodesic in a bounded way, then the original witnesses still witness two distinct deep components bound by the new quasi-geodesic.”
\item Construct a large figure called a \emph{cross-examiner} (\S\ref{sec:crosshair}, \S\ref{sec:witness-sep}); “attaching paths to the cross-examiner and applying the witness protection lemma allows us to construct closed loops which coarsely bound regions of $X$ in a controlled way,” compared to “Jordan curves in the plane.”
\item “Combining this with an inequality of Varopoulos,” in \S\ref{sec:jurisdiction} the paper “establish[es] a useful criterion for showing that $X$ has quadratic growth.”
\item Study separation for \emph{quasi-circles} and \emph{truncated quasi-circles} (\S\ref{sec:circles}) and apply the criterion to show “either all (truncated) quasi-circles coarsely bound regions of at most uniformly bounded depth, regardless of length, or $X$ has quadratic growth,” i.e. truncated quasi-circles have “\emph{limited jurisdiction}.”
\item In \S\ref{sec:final}, apply Arzel\`a--Ascoli to “construct a sequence of ‘nested’ truncated quasi-circles lying along a bi-infinite quasi-geodesic,” and observe that this “taken together with the witness protection lemma means the truncated quasi-circles cannot possibly have limited jurisdiction,” forcing “that $X$ has quadratic growth,” which completes the non-hyperbolic case.
\end{itemize}

